---
// Layered stacks converge in rounds: a cluster and a chart as a round-annotated code panel
// (global.css, `.codeblock.rounds`), what each round does, and the loop as five steps. Keeps the
// `#product` anchor. The HCL is after turf-examples/terraform/kubernetes/kind-helm, with the chart
// swapped for cert-manager as the ngc-stack example installs it; the round labels are what
// `turf up` computes for it.
---

<section class="band" id="product">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="band-head">
      <h2>Layered stacks converge in rounds.</h2>
      <p>A cluster, then the chart that runs on it. One configuration, one <code>turf up</code>: Turf
        plans what it can know, applies it, and plans again, with an approval point at every round.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="feature">
      <div class="codeblock rounds">
        <div class="cb-head">
          <span>main.tf · a cluster, then a chart</span>
          <span class="cb-legend">each round planned &amp; approved</span>
        </div>
        <div class="rounds-body">
          <div class="round round-1">
            <div class="round-tag">
              <span class="round-name">Round 1 · create</span>
              <span class="round-eff"><span class="mk">+</span> kind_cluster.demo</span>
            </div>
<pre><span class="cm"># A local Kubernetes cluster, as Docker containers.</span>
<span class="kw">resource</span> <span class="str">"kind_cluster"</span> <span class="str">"demo"</span> &#123;
  name           = <span class="str">"$&#123;var.cluster_name&#125;-$&#123;var.generation&#125;"</span>
  node_image     = var.node_image
  wait_for_ready = true
&#125;</pre>
          </div>
          <div class="round round-2">
            <div class="round-tag">
              <span class="round-name">Round 2 · create</span>
              <span class="round-eff"><span class="mk">+</span> helm_release.cert_manager</span>
            </div>
<pre><span class="cm"># The helm provider binds to the cluster's endpoint, unknown</span>
<span class="cm"># until round 1 applied. Turf configures it here, then plans</span>
<span class="cm"># the release.</span>
<span class="kw">provider</span> <span class="str">"helm"</span> &#123;
  kubernetes = &#123;
    host                   = kind_cluster.demo.endpoint
    client_certificate     = kind_cluster.demo.client_certificate
    client_key             = kind_cluster.demo.client_key
    cluster_ca_certificate = kind_cluster.demo.cluster_ca_certificate
  &#125;
&#125;

<span class="kw">resource</span> <span class="str">"helm_release"</span> <span class="str">"cert_manager"</span> &#123;
  name             = <span class="str">"cert-manager"</span>
  repository       = <span class="str">"https://charts.jetstack.io"</span>
  chart            = <span class="str">"cert-manager"</span>
  version          = <span class="str">"v1.21.2"</span>
  namespace        = <span class="str">"cert-manager"</span>
  create_namespace = true
  set  = [&#123; name = <span class="str">"crds.enabled"</span>, value = <span class="str">"true"</span> &#125;]
  wait = true
&#125;</pre>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="cb-note">after turf-examples/terraform/kubernetes/kind-helm, the chart swapped for cert-manager as the ngc-stack example installs it</div>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h3>A round plans what it can know.</h3>
        <p><b>Round 1</b> creates the cluster. The helm provider reads the cluster’s endpoint, unknown
          until then, so Turf defers the release instead of failing, applies what it can, and plans
          again.</p>
        <p><b>Round 2</b> configures the provider from the applied endpoint and syncs the release
          through Helm itself: a true Helm release, CRDs and hooks included, that <code>helm list</code>
          sees. Nothing is left deferred, so the run ends: converged, from empty, in one command.</p>
        <ul class="ticklist">
          <li><span class="g">✓</span><div><b>No <code>-target</code>, no staged applies</b> <span>— and no edits to
            the <code>.tf</code>: the file converges as written.</span></div></li>
          <li><span class="g">✓</span><div><b>An approval point on every round</b> <span>— each round’s plan is
            reviewed, by you or by policy, before it applies.</span></div></li>
          <li><span class="g">✓</span><div><b>A run that can’t progress says so</b> <span>— it ends
            <code>stalled</code> and names what it is waiting on.</span></div></li>
          <li><span class="g">✓</span><div><b>Teardown in reverse</b> <span>— <code>turf destroy</code> removes the
            release through the cluster before the cluster goes.</span></div></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step">
        <span class="n">step 1</span>
        <h3>Declare intent</h3>
        <p>Natural language, a diagram, or Terraform HCL — desired state always lands in a reviewable
        configuration directory.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <span class="n">step 2</span>
        <h3>Plan</h3>
        <p>Turf produces a standard Terraform plan of the whole directory: resources, actions, and what
        this round defers.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="step approve">
        <span class="n">step 3 · the approval point</span>
        <h3>You approve</h3>
        <p>People and policy checks sign off on the plan. Nothing is applied until this plan, and no
        other, is approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <span class="n">step 4</span>
        <h3>Apply, with the agent overseeing</h3>
        <p>The engine applies the approved plan. A turf action stops it for a check, a backup or a
        question — the agent, and you, step in there.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <span class="n">step 5</span>
        <h3>Replan &amp; converge</h3>
        <p>Deferred work — the provider that couldn’t be configured yet — loops back through a fresh plan
        until the graph settles.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="loopnote"><span class="arr">↻</span> step 5 feeds back into step 2 — every round crosses the approval point again</p>
  </div>
</section>
